                <h2 id="contracts-too">#3. Contracts carry the same risk</h2>

                <p>
                    Most freelancers think about proposals when this topic comes
                    up. Contracts are just as exposed &mdash; and the stakes are
                    higher.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A contract you send carries your Author field and ModDate.
                    But it also reveals something more sensitive: the creation
                    date. If a contract's CreationDate shows it was created
                    three years ago and barely modified, a counterparty can
                    infer it's a boilerplate you've used dozens of times. That
                    shifts the negotiation dynamic before a single clause is
                    discussed.
                </p>

                <p>
                    More concretely &mdash; if you used a contract template
                    originally drafted by a solicitor or legal service, the
                    Author field may still contain their name and company.
                    You've just inadvertently disclosed your legal provider to a
                    counterparty. Not a catastrophe, but not information you
                    would have chosen to share.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If your contract workflow involves sending and receiving
                    signed versions, consider the full chain. Every iteration of
                    the document accumulates metadata. See our guide on <a
                        href="/blog/redaction-vs-metadata-removal"
                        >redaction vs metadata removal</a
                    > for how these two operations interact in document exchange workflows.
                </p>

